You are given a rooted tree consisting of n nodes. The nodes are numbered 1,2,...,n, and node 1 is the root. Each node has a value.
Your task is to process following types of queries:

change the value of node s to x
calculate the sum of values on the path from the root to node s

Input:
The first input line contains two integers n and q: the number of nodes and queries. The nodes are numbered 1,2,...,n.
The next line has n integers v_1,v_2,...,v_n: the value of each node.
Then there are n-1 lines describing the edges. Each line contains two integers a and b: there is an edge between nodes a and b.
Finally, there are q lines describing the queries. Each query is either of the form "1 s x" or "2 s".

Output:
Print the answer to each query of type 2.

Constraints:
1<=n, q<=2*10^5
1<=a,b, s<=n
1<=v_i, x<=10^9

Strategy:
We use the strategy previously used to do efficient range updates on an array. Instead of keeping the total sums we keep the differences. We can then store the differences in a segment tree which will sum them up correctly for each path. We will flatten the tree and use the ordering as in Subtree Queries (a dfs with a counter to always do a whole subtree at once and then moving on). Then we use the difference array strategy. We do this by assigning each node a + value if we want to start adding there and a - value if we want to stop adding or decrease the amount we add by. My mental model is that each node in the flattened tree has the + part if we add that amount to all the nodes in its subtree which will very elegantly nest the values for all the smaller and smaller subtrees until we get to the leaves. Then, the second part is where we store the - part, we do this in the first value after the subtree in our order, which by the model in subtree queries is out[idx]. Then the updating, since we keep track of differences, maps out to finding the delta between what the node we are updating has now and what we want there to be and we let this delta bubble all the way up the tree to update the other counts. This is not so simple though since the nodes in the segment tree do not hold the values that the nodes in the actual tree hold, therefore we need a separate array to keep track of current nodes. This makes the initialization a bit complex since we need to inialize the tree while the node array has all 0s so we don't incorrectly count. Then for the queries we just need to find the correct range and this is the most beautiful part of this. We run a query from 0 to in[idx]+1. This makes us take into account all the nodes which have position in the dfs traversal from 0 to in[idx]. I imagine this as having the whole big tree which can be divided into many subtrees which are nested inside each other. Now the idea is that the only nodes the count of which will not get cancelled are the nodes which are on the path from root to idx. This is because once we are in a subtree we are permanently locked in it until we explore all of it therefore we have only 2 options: our target is in that subtree or we will explore all of it and move on which will cancel out all the counts since the first node after it will zero that part of the count out. Therefore when traversing the only nodes which will ever get counted must be the nodes on our path since they are the ones which create the nested subtree structure (a path) at the end of which is our target. For all the other ones we will just backtrack and cancel the count. This basically recursively (just like the dfs) gets us our number. To me it seems like a dfs with backtracking inside a segment tree. Now the query for the flattened array works for the exact same reason. It is literally a flattened dfs. For each query, if type 2, we output what the segment tree returns.

The second approach that works and is arguably better is using a fenwick tree. A fenwick tree works on the basis of creating any possible prefix by using power of 2 sized blocks and combining them to get to its target and then doing prefix(max(a,b))-prefix(min(a,b)) to get its range. It does not seem to change much compared to my approach logically but does use only N memory compared to the segment tree which uses 2N, there is also no branching like the segment has in the query function which makes it faster. The logic of calculating the delta for the updates is the same and querying by using the blocks Mr. Fenwick has developed is also using the flattened array but we use the x & -x trick which gets us the LSB set to 1. Which gives us the size of the block while the actual full number (idx) gives us the ending point of that block. It can be proven that the blocks will perfectly fill up the range space while not being redundant. This is because of the nesting property. My model is that this is like the 1/2^n series (1+1/2+1/4...) but we can start this series at any point and we do 1+2+4 or possibly something like 8+2+1. The theory guarantees we can get any range out of this is we size the blocks with x & -x and make them end at the number of their index. Here we don't even need to use the property of subtracting prefix sum since we start at the root (0). We output that. Note: we need to use 1 based indexing since 0 & 0 = 0 which would loop infinitely in the loop logic since we would be adding 0.